    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d131">

                <pb facs="0128" n="124" sameAs="frus:frus1938v03/pg124"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94119/406: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Hankow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-03-16T11:00:00+08:00">March 16, 1938—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 16—10:10 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>163. Department’s 87, February 28, 6 p.m. Doctor J. Leighton Stuart<anchor n="92"
                        xml:id="d131-f1"/> arrived in Hankow on March 11. I saw Minister for Foreign
                        Affairs<anchor n="93" xml:id="d131-f2"/> afternoon March 12 and communicated
                    to him Department’s message. Minister for Foreign Affairs stated that he
                    thoroughly understood and said that he would convey information to members of
                    Government who might be approached by Stuart. I saw Stuart on March 15. He
                    stated that he had had an interview with the Generalissimo but had not
                    communicated Wang Keh Min’s<anchor n="94" xml:id="d131-f3"/> message as he
                    discovered very quickly that there was no basis for any hope of mediation.
                    Stuart said that his visit to Hankow had convinced him that peace between China
                    and Japan was not yet possible.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Peiping and Shanghai. Shanghai please repeat to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d131-f1" n="92" xml:id="d131-f1-content">
                <p>American, president of Yenching University, near Peiping.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d131-f2" n="93" xml:id="d131-f2-content">
                <p>Wang Chung-hui.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d131-f3" n="94" xml:id="d131-f3-content">
                <p>Head of Japanese-sponsored regime at Peiping.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
